# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 3, ATTEMPT 18 on coolify - a RE-QUESTION routed back from measured runs.
# ANCHOR KEPT (`Server`, precision 0.15, lowest of nine profiled). GOLD KEPT, unchanged, all
# fifteen scored rows and four anchors, so this cycle moves exactly ONE thing: the question -
# and inside the question, only STEP 2. Step 1 is byte-identical to a17, which is the first
# attempt in this whole campaign whose sense arm actually called `sense_blast`.
#
# TRAJECTORY (every previous attempt, baseline / ours on `dependents`, quoted from its own
# minibench read; the window is plain arm <= 0.50 AND ours >= +0.50 above it):
#     a1  0.9444 / 1.0000  CEILING   class teardown, "everything that must be reviewed"
#     a2  0.8462 / 0.8462  CEILING   the layer one hop back (anchor swapped, and swapped back)
#     a3  0.3571 / 0.5714  FLOOR-OK  identity scrap, near edge excluded
#     a4  0.9286 / 0.5714  CEILING   same axis, six value-shapes named in the ask
#     a5  0.0000 / 0.3333  FLOOR-OK  exclusion sharpened; baseline died at the wall
#     a6  0.7500 / 0.9167  CEILING   near edge required as a first pass inside step 2
#     a7  void   / 0.7500  NEITHER   ground moved into step 1; baseline died in the ground
#     a8  0.0000 / 0.7500  FLOOR-OK  ground as counts; baseline hard-capped, ours never
#                                    called blast - both halves void
#     a9  0.9167 / 0.8333  CEILING   keepsake axis + a "holds no machine" selection test
#     a10 0.8000 / 0.9333  CEILING   selection rule; baseline FINISHED with 164s unspent
#     a11 0.9286 / 1.0000  CEILING   population answer; baseline 13 of 14, 19-search ladder
#     a12 0.7857 / 0.7857  CEILING   scrap axis restored, gold checked against the payload
#     a14 0.2667 / 0.2667  NEITHER   gold moved off "Server"-named paths, ground de-enumerated
#                                    in step 1. THE BASELINE HALF LANDED: 0.2667 with 146s of
#                                    556s UNSPENT - the first FINISHED sub-floor baseline on
#                                    this repo since a3, and the ceiling was open at +0.7333.
#                                    OURS FAILED: zero `sense_blast` calls in either run, four
#                                    MCP calls each (`sense_status`, two `sense_search` on
#                                    concept strings, one `sense_graph` at `findCached`), then
#                                    `grep-fallback in 2/2`. Every dependents row ours cited is
#                                    CITED-NOT-RETURNED - it scored 0.2667 by doing exactly
#                                    what the baseline did.
#     a15 void   / 0.0000  NEITHER   step 2 opened straight on "take it as settled that the
#                                    whole record is being replaced"; the baseline arm is
#                                    recorded null in cycles.jsonl, so the pair is void.
#     a16 0.3333 / 0.2000  NEITHER   the file benched as sha256:bb97fec3ea39e11d, identical to
#                                    the archived scenario under .versions/ (diff, this
#                                    session). BASELINE HALF LANDED AGAIN: 5 of 15, FINISHED
#                                    (exit 0, watchdog None, 512 of 570s), on a 78-call,
#                                    60-search sweep ladder - it did NOT assemble the set, and
#                                    the ceiling above it is +0.6667.
#                                    OURS FAILED AGAIN, and this time legibly: three MCP calls
#                                    (`sense_search` on a concept string, `sense_status`, then
#                                    `sense_graph {"symbol":"App\\Models\\Server","depth":1,
#                                    "direction":"callees"}`), zero `tools/call` for
#                                    `sense_blast`, then 46 greps. It NAMED the type - a14/a15
#                                    never did - and then expanded in the WRONG DIRECTION, one
#                                    hop, outbound. 3 of 15, every cited row
#                                    CITED-NOT-RETURNED, `grep-fallback in 1/1`.
#     a17 0.2000 / 0.4667  FLOOR-OK  the a17 step-1 rewrite WORKED, on the one thing it aimed
#                                    at: the sense arm called `sense_blast` on `Server` /
#                                    `app/Models/Server.php` at `max_hops: 4` and was handed
#                                    35,379 chars - the FIRST attempt in eighteen with a real
#                                    blast call in it, after a8/a14/a15/a16 made zero.
#                                    BASELINE HALF PASSED, hardest yet: 3 of 15 = 0.2000,
#                                    exit 124, `hard_cap_timeout`, wall 315 of 315. Its route
#                                    was 40 calls / 30 searches, re-cutting one token family
#                                    (`server_uuid|serverUuid|server_id|serverId|server->uuid`)
#                                    against ever-narrower directories, every hit list
#                                    truncated (`| head -20`, `-40`, `-60`), still opening
#                                    single files by hand at call 39. It did NOT assemble the
#                                    set. Ceiling above it: +0.8000.
#                                    OURS FAILED, AND THE FAILURE IS NOW ONE STEP LATER AND
#                                    ONE STEP NARROWER: 7 of 15, exit 0, watchdog None, wall
#                                    262 of 480 - it stopped with 218 SECONDS UNSPENT. All
#                                    EIGHT missed rows were IN the payload it was shown
#                                    (checked path by path in the a17 minibench read);
#                                    `tool_use_audit` reported "no misuse flags" and
#                                    `transcript_miss` mined `resolver-sev=0`, so nothing was
#                                    withheld by the index and nothing was cut off by the
#                                    wall. It was handed the set and returned the subset it
#                                    liked. (`grep-fallback in 1/1` alongside, the same
#                                    triage-abandonment seen from the other side.)
#
#   WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE. The floor side, for
#   the fourth cycle running, and by less than any previous draft has had to move. a14, a15,
#   a16 and a17 are all floor-side, and the baseline is now landing lower each time it finishes
#   the exam (0.2667 finished, 0.3333 finished, 0.2000 hard-capped). Gold is untouched, so
#   nothing in this draft moves the baseline by design. Ours must go from 0.4667 to at or above
#   0.70 - eleven of fifteen against the seven it cited - which is what +0.50 over a 0.2000
#   baseline demands, and the ceiling is open at +0.8000. That is the smallest gap any cycle on
#   this repo has been asked to close.
#
# THE ONE VARIABLE: STEP 2 NOW SCORES THE PASS, NOT THE PICKS. STEP 1 IS BYTE-IDENTICAL.
#   a17 settled the retrieval question and left exactly one thing broken, and for the first
#   time it is not a retrieval failure at all. The arm HELD the answer: eight of eight missed
#   rows were inside the payload it was shown, the index withheld nothing (`resolver-sev=0`),
#   and it closed with 218 of 480 seconds unspent. It read a set and reported the part of it it
#   was most confident about. Under a17's step 2 that was a rational reading - the step asked
#   for "every place that took one identifying piece off a machine", so a short list of certain
#   places is a well-formed answer to it, and a member of the set never mentioned is invisible
#   rather than wrong.
#   So step 2 is rewritten to make the PASS the deliverable and silence a failure, and nothing
#   else in the scenario changes:
#     (i)   the set is named as a fixed thing with an edge, established once in the first part,
#           and this part is spending it in order rather than searching again;
#     (ii)  EVERY member of it gets a ruling, and there are exactly two rulings: a place where
#           a piece is kept, which gets its three parts in full, or the ground, which gets its
#           location and the few words that say why - a member passed over in silence is a
#           WRONG answer, not an omission, because it cannot be told apart from one never
#           reached;
#     (iii) confidence is explicitly refused as a filter: an uncertain member is ruled with the
#           doubt written beside it, never dropped;
#     (iv)  closing while a member is unruled and working time remains is named as the specific
#           way this part gets failed - a17's exact exit.
#   The three-part answer shape, the "what breaks" framing, the ground exclusion, the at-the-
#   line test and the paragraph on how these places hide are all KEPT VERBATIM from a17. So are
#   step 1, the checks, the gold and the anchor.
#   NEGATIVE SPACE on this variable, and it is a real one. A ruling-per-member instruction can
#   cost budget: the shown payload carries 101 distinct paths, so a full ledger is 101 lines of
#   output. Two facts bound that without settling it - a17's sense arm left 218 of 480 seconds
#   unspent, and the ruled-out branch is deliberately priced at a location plus a few words, so
#   the ledger is cheap where the ground is thick. Whether it instead eats the wall is not
#   knowable from here and only the run answers; it is this draft's named risk. It could also
#   in principle help the BASELINE, which builds a set of its own - but a17's baseline never
#   reached the end of its first sweep, so a completeness instruction has nothing to complete.
#   THE RISK THE PREVIOUS READ FLAGGED IS NOW CLOSED, not carried: "a8, a14, a15 and a16 all
#   ended with zero `sense_blast` calls, this is the FOURTH ask aimed at that failure, and if
#   it fails again route to the instrument rather than to a17's successor." It did not fail
#   again. a17 called blast, at four hops, and was served every scoring row. The instrument is
#   not the diagnosis; the judgement pass is, and that is a question the question can reach.
#
# THE CITATION RANKING, RUN THIS SESSION, AND WHAT IT SAYS THIS GOLD COSTS
#   python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py coolify verticals/php-laravel/results/claude-opus-5/minibench \
#     --by-path verticals/php-laravel/scenarios/.versions
#   34 runs (baseline x16, sense x18), model claude-opus-5, 15 scenario versions merged by gold
#   PATH. Scoped to the headline model. On the fifteen rows this gold actually holds:
#     DISCRIMINATORS  Livewire/GlobalSearch.php           baseline  5/12   sense 11/13
#                     Container/ContainerStopped.php      baseline  3/13   sense  8/15
#                     Destination/New/Docker.php          baseline  4/13   sense  8/14
#     FREE, AND KEPT  Helpers/SshMultiplexingHelper.php   baseline  6/6    sense  7/8
#                     helpers/remoteProcess.php           baseline  5/5    sense  6/7
#                     Models/StandaloneDocker.php         baseline 10/15   sense 15/17
#                     (the tool prints StandaloneDocker under `contract` because older versions
#                      golded it there; in THIS gold it is `d:destination-identity-map`)
#     NEVER CITED     Project/Shared/Destination.php           baseline 0/3   sense 0/4
#                     Models/DockerCleanupExecution.php        baseline 1/4   sense 0/5
#                     Project/Shared/GetLogs.php               baseline 0/3   sense 1/4
#                     CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php  baseline 0/3   sense 1/4
#   Three free rows put a measured floor of roughly 3/15 = 0.20 under the baseline - and a17's
#   baseline landed on EXACTLY that floor and nowhere above it, citing remoteProcess,
#   SshMultiplexingHelper and one more in forty calls. Four near-dead rows dilute the group and
#   put a practical ceiling near 12/15 on ours.
#   THE PRE-REGISTERED CUT DID NOT FIRE. a17's header recorded, before its run: "If a17's
#   baseline lands ABOVE 0.50, these three free rows are the first thing the next cycle cuts."
#   It landed at 0.2000. The condition is false, so gold is untouched, as registered. Re-golding
#   here would also spend this cycle's one variable on the half that is already passing.
#   PRE-REGISTERED AGAIN, FOR a19, RECORDED BEFORE THIS RUN: if a18's baseline stays at or
#   below 0.50 and ours still falls short of +0.50, the next move is the GOLD and not the
#   question - cut the four never-cited rows first (they can only ever dilute ours; the
#   baseline scores 1 of 4 across sixteen runs on them), and only then reconsider the three
#   free rows, whose removal moves the baseline down rather than ours up.
#
# WHAT a14, a16 AND a17 SETTLED, SO THIS DRAFT DOES NOT RE-SPEND IT
#   VARIABLE 1 (gold entirely off "Server"-named paths) WORKED and is kept verbatim: a12's
#   baseline cited 11 of 14 on a gold that was 8 of 14 inside "Server"-named paths; a14's cited
#   4 of 15 on this one, which is 0 of 15, at the same 46-48 call budget and the same sweep
#   style. Gold is therefore UNCHANGED - all nineteen rows, same ids, same lines. Re-golding
#   under n=1 per arm would also break the ~5-runs-per-arm floor the ranking needs.
#   VARIABLE 2 (step 1 no longer rolls out the working ground) is kept: a14's baseline FINISHED
#   with 146s spare, so step 1 is not what starves it. Step 1's second half keeps the
#   shape-not-roll wording and adds only the named-subject frame.
#   THE PAYLOAD QUESTION a14 COULD NOT ANSWER IS NOW ANSWERED (it left eleven of nineteen rows
#   at `neither` and asked whether they are servable at all). Run this session:
#     python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'
#     31419 chars, 101 distinct paths in the shown payload; all FIFTEEN scored paths and three
#     of the four anchor paths intersect it (the fourth is app/Models/Server.php, the anchor's
#     own file, which a blast of Server does not list as a dependent of itself).
#   So the ten unreached `dependents` rows are NOT unreachable: the instrument carries every
#   one of them and the arm never opened it. That is what makes this a sense-side cycle.
#   VARIABLE 3 (a17's step 1: impact-shaped, near-edge-refused, established in one sweep) WORKED
#   and is kept BYTE-IDENTICAL. It is the only wording in eighteen attempts that has produced a
#   `sense_blast` call on the anchor, and it produced one at four hops carrying every scoring
#   row. Touching step 1 this cycle would put the one thing that finally works back at risk to
#   buy nothing: a17's failure is downstream of it, with the payload already in hand and a third
#   of the wall unspent.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Per place: WHICH piece of identity was kept, HOW LONG it outlives the machine object
#   (one request, one stored row, or outside this product entirely), and WHAT THAT PLACE
#   STARTS GETTING WRONG once the piece stops resolving - and those three differ between lines
#   whose text is nearly identical. A number on a cleanup-execution row is orphaned by a
#   delete; the same number on a deployment queue row is read back later to cancel a build on
#   the wrong machine; a handle in a mux cache key survives a restart and reuses a connection
#   to a machine that has been replaced; a handle in a webhook body has already left the
#   product and cannot be corrected at all; a number arriving on a broadcast event is resolved
#   with firstOrFail and takes the listener down rather than skipping. A search prints the
#   occurrence; it cannot print the lifetime or the failure.
#   This is NOT the retention kind, and that is a run, not a preference:
#     python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#       -> "0 candidate(s) above the bar", all twelve anchors "rows 0  files 0  vias 0".
#   Zero rings, so "what HOLDS X" is off the menu on this repo.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py). `Server` re-profiled THIS session and quoted from that
# run; the other eight were profiled in the a17 session and are carried forward unchanged.
#   Server        app caller files 45 | SCATTER 23 | 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15
#   StandaloneDocker  5 | SCATTER  5 | 'StandaloneDocker' in 26 files            PRECISION=0.19
#   Team             26 | SCATTER 14 | 'Team' in 104 app files                   PRECISION=0.25
#   Application      35 | SCATTER 18 | 'Application' in 142 app files            PRECISION=0.25
#   Service          24 | SCATTER 11 | 'Service' in 93 app files                 PRECISION=0.26
#   ApplicationDeploymentQueue 7 | SCATTER 6 | in 18 files                       PRECISION=0.39
#   PrivateKey       19 | SCATTER 12 | 'PrivateKey' in 42 app files              PRECISION=0.45
#   Environment      46 | SCATTER 33 | 'Environment' in 79 app files             PRECISION=0.58
#   EnvironmentVariable 4 | SCATTER 4 | in 28 files                              PRECISION=0.14
#   PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS. `Server` is lowest among anchors that can yield twelve
#   dependent files across six areas (EnvironmentVariable is lower at 0.14 but has four caller
#   files, so it cannot), and it is the anchor thirteen cycles of measurement attach to.
#   ANCHOR KEPT.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN THIS SESSION, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'
#   No `min_confidence` passed. 31419 chars; 46 `direct_callers` + 89 `affected_via_composition`,
#   union of distinct paths = 101. All fifteen scored paths and all four anchor paths were
#   intersected against that union path by path; every one is inside it.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Server --json)
#   Closed-book recall of the anchor's own member names is 0.533 (57 of 107 named), so the
#   model can recite what a machine record offers. Gold-time constraint, not an admission
#   verdict: not one scored row is a member of the anchor. Every scored row is a line-level
#   fact in a different file - a fillable entry, an assignment into component state, a cache
#   key, a pivot attach, a webhook field - and none appears in the probe's answer.
#   Churn-dating cannot discriminate on this clone: it is a single pinned snapshot and
#   `git log -1 --format=%as` returns 2026-07-30 for every file in it.
#
# NEGATIVE SPACE - what I did NOT check. I did not measure whether the fifteen rows are
# reachable by one composite grep; a covering pattern for parts of this family plainly exists
# (`server_id|server_uuid|selectedServer|Cache::`), and it is recorded here and decides
# nothing, per NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE. What the ranking says is that the baseline runs that
# pattern and still discards these rows as noise, because the files are about containers,
# search, wizards, logs and cleanup records. Whether it discards them again is the run.
# WHAT I RAN THIS SESSION, AND WHAT I DID NOT, stated rather than implied.
# RAN: every cycle record on disk (`cycles.1/2.jsonl` + `loop/cycles.jsonl`, eighteen attempts,
# not only the last); the a17 minibench read end to end; `inverse_frequency.py` over the
# headline model's own results root (34 runs, 15 versions merged by path, quoted above);
# `ring_sweep.py --top 12` (0 candidates, all twelve anchors rows 0 / files 0 / vias 0);
# `seam_hunt.py Server --propose` (45 app caller files, SCATTER 23, 'Server' in 295 app files,
# PRECISION=0.15, verdict SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY); `mcp_probe.py` on `sense_blast {"symbol":
# "Server"}` with no `min_confidence` (31419 chars, all fifteen scored paths intersected one by
# one, all fifteen present); and EIGHT gold lines opened by hand this session -
# DeploymentNavbar:100, CloneMe:88, DockerCleanupExecution:10, StandaloneDocker:135,
# GetLogs:127, remoteProcess.php:49, Select.php:409, Shared/Destination.php:130 - all eight
# verbatim against their `relation` text. Together with a17's five (ContainerStopped:114,
# GlobalSearch:1208, SshMultiplexingHelper:347, CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification:18,
# docker.php:1252) that is thirteen of fifteen hand-read across two sessions.
# NOT RUN: the memorization probe. Gold is byte-identical to a16's and a17's, which were
# probed, against the same pinned snapshot, so a re-run measures the same rows against the same
# clone. NOT MEASURED: whether the fifteen rows are reachable by one composite grep. A covering
# pattern for parts of this family plainly exists (`server_id|server_uuid|selectedServer|
# Cache::`); it is recorded here and it decides nothing, per NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE - and
# a17's baseline ran that exact family at call 10 and reached 3 of 15 anyway. NOT VERIFIED, and
# unverifiable from here: whether a ruling-per-member instruction makes the arm finish the set
# or makes it spend the wall on the ground. That is the whole question this draft asks, and
# only the two-arm run answers it.
# ============================================================================
name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product
  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a
  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of
  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a
  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a
  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is
  read back tomorrow.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment
  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at
  no machine, without a single line of them changing.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it.
      Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these
      machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give
      the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a
      single subject rather than as a word to look for.
      Then two things about it, and the first is the larger.
      First, the identity itself, traced end to end. What identifies one of these machines;
      where the identifying fields are set as the record is written; where a lookup by identity
      is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store, what empties that cache and
      when; where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it,
      including any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; and
      what the removal path does, in order, and what it does not clean up behind it. Every
      routine you name gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without
      that location does not count.
      Second, and briefly: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as
      a roll. Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses -
      the store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none
      of that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the
      other direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE
      that type changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far
      side is the part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes
      that type's name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller
      and easier part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed
      something a near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the
      type's name anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step
      from the type. An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping
      there is the specific way this part gets failed.
      Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product
      and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the
      areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how
      far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking
      for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as
      one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the second part of this
      ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice.
      Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the
      first half of this answer to keep meaning what it means today.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, cache, seed and tear it down, each located at file:line, plus the shape of the population a change to that type would land on - the areas it reaches, near edge and far side, named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one
      go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This
      part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each
      line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this
      gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the
      only question that matters after that: what breaks.
      Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in
      front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and
      it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place that took
      one identifying piece off a machine, kept that piece after the machine itself was gone
      from its hands, and later trusts it to still mean the same machine when it comes back to
      it - a request later, a restart later, or in another system entirely. That trust is
      exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places changes by a single line
      when it does.
      Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there: is a piece being lifted off a
      machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to, or read back out
      of somewhere after the machine was gone? If instead a machine came in and the code acted
      on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out.
      One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of
      them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was
      taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a
      plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name
      you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not
      either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where
      the machine itself did not travel.
      How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is
      judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you
      established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either
      a piece is being kept there, and it gets the three parts below in full; or it is the
      ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and
      nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you
      simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here
      it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure
      you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never
      dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of
      the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a
      member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you.
      For each kept place the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line where
      the piece is taken, kept or read back, and the routine that line sits in; how long that
      piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored record, or
      outside this product entirely; and one phrase on what this place starts getting wrong the
      first time it no longer resolves to the machine it came from. Keep it short: I want the
      coverage, the lifetime and the failure, not the prose.
      Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as
      finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with its own location; then
      close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you genuinely run out of
      time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that point, rather than
      closing on what you have. A file:line with no lifetime and no failure is half an answer,
      and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place located at file:line with the routine it sits in, the lifetime of the piece it keeps and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer resolves, and each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its identity map, its obvious write path and its removal
  # ---- path. Step 1's answer. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every line below
  # ---- was opened and read this session. None of these four files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine's number, flushed at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void` which :222 `static::updated(...)` calls; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:34 `public function delete($password, $selectedActions = [])` ending at :60 `$this->server->delete();` - the removal path, the moment after which every piece kept elsewhere resolves to nothing"
  - id: c:validate-server
    group: contract
    match: [Server/ValidateServer.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Server/ValidateServer.php:26 `public function handle(Server $server)` - the machine handed in and worked on with it in hand, the canonical shape of the ground that step 2 excludes"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. Fifteen files, nine areas, one row per file. Every one
  # ---- appears in the shown `sense_blast` payload for the contract (checked path by path this
  # ---- session). NOT ONE of the fifteen paths contains "Server": the measured split is
  # ---- baseline 0.70 on Server-named paths against 0.35 off them, and this group is entirely
  # ---- off them. At every scored line there is no machine in hand - only a piece taken off one
  # ---- earlier and kept somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to.
  - id: d:container-webhook-handle
    group: dependents
    match: [Container/ContainerStopped.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:114 `'server_uuid' => $this->server->uuid,` inside `toWebhook()` at :106 - the machine's handle copied into the body of an outbound webhook that leaves this product entirely, so the piece lives in someone else's system and can never be corrected from here; rarest-cited row on the board at baseline 3/10, sense 8/11"
  - id: d:global-search-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Livewire/GlobalSearch.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/GlobalSearch.php:1208 `$this->selectedServerId = $serverId;` inside `selectServer()` at :1206 - a picked machine reduced to a number kept in the component's state, re-sent by the browser on every later request and resolved again at :1218 `Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($this->selectedServerId)` inside `loadDestinations()` at :1215; baseline 3/7, sense 8/8"
  - id: d:new-destination-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Destination/New/Docker.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php:47 `$this->serverId = (string) $this->selectedServer->id;` inside `mount()` - the number stringified into component state, resolved back on a later request at :61 `$this->servers->find($this->serverId)` and written into the created destination at :87 / :98, so the machine is gone from its hands between the two halves of the form; baseline 3/7, sense 6/8"
  - id: d:new-resource-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/New/Select.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:409 `$this->server_id = $server->id;` inside `setServer()` at :407 - the number kept in wizard state across every subsequent request of the flow, seeded from a query string at :83 and written into the created records later; baseline 3/7, sense 5/8"
  - id: d:deployment-queue-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Application/DeploymentNavbar.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Application/DeploymentNavbar.php:100 `$build_server_id = $this->application_deployment_queue->build_server_id ?? $this->application->destination->server_id;` and :101 the same for `server_id` - numbers written onto a deployment queue row when the deployment started and read back much later at :110 / :112 to find the machine a cancel must be run on"
  - id: d:tunnel-event-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php]
    relation: "app/Listeners/CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php:18 `$server_id = data_get($event, 'data.server_id');` inside `handle()` at :16 - a bare number arriving on a broadcast event raised by another process, resolved at :21 `Server::where('id', $server_id)->firstOrFail()`, so a number that no longer means the same machine takes the listener down rather than skipping"
  - id: d:clone-state-id
